[THOUGHT] The physical exam reveals a classic “double bubble” sign on inspection — distended upper abdomen with concave lower abdomen — highly suggestive of proximal duodenal obstruction (e.g., duodenal atresia or annular pancreas). Normal vital signs and bowel sounds are reassuring but do not rule out obstruction. However, critical gaps remain: absence of data on meconium passage (key for ruling out imperforate anus or Hirschsprung), abdominal tenderness/rigidity (to assess for perforation or peritonitis), and neurologic/physiologic stability markers (alertness, tone, capillary refill) — all essential to triage urgency and narrow differentials. Since these were not captured in the prior exam, I must explicitly request them now — they fall under domain #7 (targeted physical examination) and are clinically indispensable.

[TEST] Perform focused reassessment of:  
- Presence or absence of meconium or stool in the rectum (digital rectal exam);  
- Abdominal tenderness and rigidity (gentle palpation);  
- Infant’s alertness (e.g., spontaneous eye opening, response to stimulation);  
- Muscle tone (e.g., flexor tone in arms/legs, resistance to extension);  
- Capillary refill time (press on sternum or toe);  
- Respiratory effort (e.g., grunting, nasal flaring, subcostal recession).

[THOUGHT] Repeated attempts to elicit history from the caregiver have yielded “no information” for feeding, meconium, systemic symptoms, Apgars, medications, and family history. At this point, further subjective questioning is unlikely to yield new data. I have confirmed the key objective findings: bilious vomiting since day 1, distended upper abdomen with concave lower abdomen, and radiographic “double bubble” — all highly specific for duodenal atresia. To complete the Evidence Framework, I must still address domain #3 (associated symptoms) *objectively*: many neonates with duodenal atresia have comorbid anomalies best detected by targeted screening exams. The most actionable next step is a cardiac auscultation (to detect murmurs suggesting CHD, present in ~25–30% of cases) and assessment for dysmorphic features (e.g., upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds) — both quick, bedside, and high-yield. This fulfills domain #7 (targeted physical exam) more completely and informs urgency of genetic evaluation.

[TEST] Perform focused physical examination for:  
- Cardiac auscultation (listening for murmurs, gallops, or abnormal splitting);  
- Dysmorphic facial features (e.g., upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, low-set ears, flat nasal bridge);  
- Examination of hands (for single palmar crease) and feet (for wide sandal gap, clinodactyly).

